{
  "abstract": "We reassess whether the weak mixing angle provides a redundant test of the selected MTT gauge profile. In grand-unified hypercharge normalization, let r21 be the ratio I2/I1, equal to the squared U(1)-to-SU(2) gauge-coupling ratio. The squared sine of the weak mixing angle is then 3 r21 divided by 5 + 3 r21 at the same scale and in the same scheme. The selected SMDR profile at the top-mass scale gives 0.2346644 plus or minus 0.0000433. Because r21 itself comes from the measured electroweak gauge profile, this equality is an algebraic round trip, not a held-out prediction. Supplying an absolute SU(2) coupling from the Fermi constant and W mass does not remove that circularity. A genuine redundancy test requires an MTT source theorem selecting r21 without electroweak gauge data, followed by independent common-scheme transport.",
